📘 Data Structures & Algorithms
🔗 Linked List — Deep Dive Notes
1️⃣ What is a Linked List?

A Linked List is a linear data structure where elements (nodes) are stored non-contiguously in memory.
Each node contains:

Data

Reference (pointer) to the next node

📌 Unlike arrays, linked lists do not require continuous memory allocation.

2️⃣ Why Linked List? (Array vs Linked List)
Feature	Array	Linked List
Memory	Contiguous	Non-contiguous
Size	Fixed	Dynamic
Insertion	Costly (O(n))	Efficient (O(1))
Deletion	Costly (O(n))	Efficient (O(1))
Access	O(1)	O(n)

📌 Use Linked List when:

Frequent insertions/deletions

Size not known in advance

3️⃣ Types of Linked List
🔹 1. Singly Linked List

Each node points to the next node only.

HEAD → [10 | next] → [20 | next] → [30 | null]

🔹 2. Doubly Linked List

Each node has previous & next pointers.

null ← [10] ⇄ [20] ⇄ [30] → null


✔ Easier backward traversal
❌ Extra memory usage

🔹 3. Circular Linked List

Last node points back to the head.

HEAD → 10 → 20 → 30 ↺


✔ Used in scheduling, round-robin systems

4️⃣ Node Structure (Java Example)
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data){
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

5️⃣ Core Operations
🔸 Insert Operations
a) Insert at Head

Create new node

Point new node to current head

Update head

⏱ Time: O(1)

b) Insert at Tail

Traverse till last node

Attach new node

⏱ Time: O(n)
(⏱ O(1) if tail pointer exists)

c) Insert at Position

Traverse till (pos-1)

Adjust pointers

⏱ Time: O(n)

🔸 Delete Operations
a) Delete Head

Move head to next node

⏱ Time: O(1)

b) Delete Tail

Traverse till second-last node

Set next = null

⏱ Time: O(n)

c) Delete by Value

Search value

Adjust links

⏱ Time: O(n)

6️⃣ Traversal
Node temp = head;
while(temp != null){
    System.out.print(temp.data + " ");
    temp = temp.next;
}


⏱ Time: O(n)

7️⃣ Important Interview Problems (Must Know)
🔥 1. Reverse a Linked List

Iterative Approach

Use prev, curr, next

Reverse links step-by-step

⏱ Time: O(n)
📦 Space: O(1)

🔥 2. Detect Cycle (Floyd’s Algorithm)

Slow pointer (1 step)

Fast pointer (2 steps)

If they meet → cycle exists

⏱ Time: O(n)
📦 Space: O(1)

🔥 3. Find Middle of Linked List

Slow & fast pointer technique

✔ Used in merge sort
✔ Used in palindrome check

🔥 4. Remove Nth Node from End

Two pointer technique

Maintain distance = n

🔥 5. Merge Two Sorted Linked Lists

Compare nodes

Build sorted result

🔥 6. Check Palindrome Linked List

Steps:

Find middle

Reverse second half

Compare both halves

